<first_data>
To create a messaging app with the described feature using Dart, you'll need to use the Flutter framework for the app's UI and Firebase Firestore for the messaging backend. Here's a high-level overview of how to approach building this app:

1. Set up Flutter and Firebase:
   - First, make sure you have Flutter installed and set up on your development machine: https://flutter.dev/docs/get-started/install
   - Then, set up a new Firebase project and add the necessary dependencies for Firebase Firestore: https://firebase.flutter.dev/docs/overview

2. Create a basic UI for the messaging app:
   - You'll need a list view to display messages, a text input field to compose messages, and a button to send messages.

3. Implement user authentication:
   - Use Firebase Authentication to handle user registration and login. You can follow this guide: https://firebase.flutter.dev/docs/auth/usage

4. Implement messaging functionality:
   - When a message is sent, save it to Firestore with a unique ID, sender ID, recipient ID, a timestamp, and a boolean flag to indicate if the message has been unsent.
   - Listen for new messages from Firestore and display them in the list view. If the message has the unsent flag set to true, do not display it.

5. Implement system tray notifications:
   - Use the flutter_local_notifications package to show notifications in the system tray: https://pub.dev/packages/flutter_local_notifications
   - When a new message is received, create a notification with the message content and display it in the system tray.
   - If the unsent flag is set to true for a message, remove the corresponding notification from the system tray.

6. Implement the unsend feature:
   - When a user chooses to unsend a message, update the message document in Firestore to set the unsent flag to true.
   - In your message listener, update the UI and system tray notifications accordingly when the unsent flag is set to true.

This is just a high-level overview to get you started. There are many more details to handle, but this should give you a good foundation for building your messaging app with Dart and Flutter.
</first_data>

